    "---\n",
    "## 7. Discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "### Data Analysis — Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "| Observation | Detail |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Dataset size | 4,894 records across 103 account categories |\n",
    "| Class imbalance | Top class has 1,179 samples; 16 classes have only 1 sample |\n",
    "| Text overlap | ~83% of records: `itemName == itemDescription` — descriptions are repeated |\n",
    "| Vendor predictiveness | ~60% of vendors always bill to the same account — strong categorical signal |\n",
    "| Leading date codes | Many descriptions start with booking codes like `0226`, `1225` — stripped as noise |\n",
    "| Amount distribution | Wide range spanning several orders of magnitude; log-transform stabilises |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Methodology\n",
    "\n",
    "**Feature engineering decisions:**\n",
    "- Combined `itemName` + `itemDescription` into one field; when they are identical (83% of records), used only once to avoid artificial TF boost\n",
    "- Stripped leading 4-digit date/period codes before vectorising\n",
    "- Used both word n-grams (1–2) and character n-grams (2–4 char_wb) — word n-grams capture full keywords; char n-grams catch partial matches in short or coded descriptions\n",
    "- One-hot encoded the top 200 vendors — the most impactful single feature change, since vendor identity is deterministic for ~60% of records\n",
    "- Log-transformed amounts and quantile-bucketed them; added an is-negative flag for credit entries\n",
    "\n",
    "**Handling class imbalance:**\n",
    "- `class_weight='balanced'` in both LR and LinearSVC — penalises misclassifying rare classes more heavily\n",
    "- Stratified train/test split preserves class ratios; singleton classes grouped under `_rare_` for stratification compatibility\n",
    "- 5-fold stratified CV gives a more reliable accuracy estimate than a single split\n",
    "\n",
    "### Strengths\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Vendor one-hot is the decisive feature.** Adding vendor identity as one-hot features over a text-only baseline gives a large jump in accuracy, reflecting the real-world pattern that most vendors always use the same account code.\n",
    "2. **Char n-grams add robustness.** When item descriptions are short or coded, character-level patterns like `subscri`, `consult`, `recr` provide signal that word-level features would miss.\n",
    "3. **LinearSVC is fast and strong.** It reaches high accuracy in seconds, scales well to the sparse high-dimensional feature matrix, and requires no probability calibration for classification.\n",
    "4. **Fully reproducible.** Fixed seeds, deterministic split, serialised artifacts.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Singleton classes.** 16 classes have exactly 1 sample — the model has no training signal for these and will always misclassify them. Their share of overall records is tiny, so they barely affect accuracy, but macro-F1 takes a hit.\n",
    "2. **Short / coded descriptions.** Many descriptions are just booking codes (`0226 Branch`). Better upstream descriptions would significantly help all models.\n",
    "3. **Vendor cold-start.** New vendors not seen during training get all-zero one-hot rows. Their predictions rely purely on the text features, which may be less accurate.\n",
    "4. **Static model.** As new expense categories and vendors are added, the model needs periodic retraining to stay current.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Ideas for Improvement\n",
    "\n",
    "| Idea | Expected Impact |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Vendor-account lookup rule (for vendors that are 100% deterministic) | High — ~40% of records could be classified without ML at all |\n",
    "| Sentence embeddings (`all-MiniLM-L6-v2`) instead of TF-IDF | Medium — richer semantics for varied descriptions |\n",
    "| Hierarchical classification (account family → specific code) | Medium — reduces confusion between adjacent categories |\n",
    "| Active learning for singleton classes | Low-to-medium — targeted data collection |\n",
    "| Confidence-threshold routing to human review | Practical — auto-classify only when probability > 0.85 |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Business Deployment Considerations\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Confidence thresholding.** Only auto-apply the predicted account when the model's top probability exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.85). Route low-confidence cases to a finance reviewer.\n",
    "- **Audit trail.** Log the model version, predicted account, and confidence score alongside each auto-classification for audit and compliance purposes.\n",
    "- **Retraining cadence.** Monitor accuracy on new transactions monthly; trigger retraining if it drops below 90%.\n",
    "- **Interpretability.** TF-IDF coefficients in Logistic Regression can be inspected to show finance teams which keywords drove a prediction, building trust in the system.\n"
   ]
  },
